y.\n","\n","For many applications, recursive splitting is a good starting point.\n","\n","## Token-Based Chunking\n","\n","Token-based chunking splits documents according to the number of tokens rather than characters.\n","\n","Why is this useful?\n","\n","Because embedding models and LLMs have token-based input limits.\n","\n","Consider:\n","\n","```\n","Text:\n","\"RAG uses vector databases to retrieve relevant context.\"\n","```\n","\n","The number of characters is not necessarily equal to the number of tokens.\n","\n","Tokenization depends on the model's tokenizer.\n","\n","For example, words like:\n","\n","```\n","ApproximateNearestNeighbor\n","```\n","\n","may be split into multiple tokens.\n","\n","Token-based chunking provides better control over input lengths."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":11,"id":"3a221177","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-03T18:51:56.891364Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-03T18:51:56.890967Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.114438Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.113424Z"},"papermill":{"duration":1.230177,"end_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.116448+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:56.886271+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Chunk 1:\n","\n","Machine learning is a subset of artificial intelligence.\n","It allows systems to learn from data.\n","Deep learning uses neural networks.\n","\n"]}],"source":["from langchain_text_splitters import TokenTextSplitter\n","\n","splitter = TokenTextSplitter(\n","    encoding_name=\"cl100k_base\",\n","    chunk_size=100,\n","    chunk_overlap=20\n",")\n","\n","chunks = splitter.split_text(text)\n","\n","for i, chunk in enumerate(chunks):\n","    print(f\"Chunk {i+1}:\")\n","    print(chunk)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ee15cfce","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.003473,"end_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.12344+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.119967+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Here:\n","\n","- `chunk_size=100` means 100 tokenizer tokens.\n","- `chunk_overlap=20` means approximately 20 tokens of overlap.\n","\n","### Important distinction\n","\n","Character-based:\n","\n","```\n","chunk_size=500\n","```\n","\n","means 500 characters.\n","\n","Token-based:\n","\n","```\n","chunk_size=500\n","```\n","\n","means 500 tokens when the splitter is configured with a tokenizer.\n","\n","The parameter name is the same, but the unit is different."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"bdbf231e","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.003393,"end_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.130901+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.127508+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Chunking Strategy Comparison\n","\n","Here is a comparison of the major techniques.\n","\n","| Strategy        | Main principle                                | Complexity  | Best use case                   |\n","| --------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------- |\n","| Fixed-size      | Split by characters or tokens                 | Low         | Basic prototypes                |\n","| Sentence-based  | Split by sentence boundaries                  | Low         | General prose                   |\n","| Recursive       | Split using hierarchical separators           | Low–Medium  | General-purpose RAG             |\n","| Token-based     | Respect tokenizer limits                      | Low–Medium  | LLM input constraints           |\n","| Semantic        | Split based on meaning changes                | High        | Topic-rich documents            |\n","| Structure-aware | Preserve document structure                   | Medium–High | PDFs, docs, websites            |\n","| Parent-child    | Retrieve small chunks, supply larger context  | High        | Complex RAG                     |\n","| Late chunking   | Contextualize before pooling chunk embeddings | High        | Long-context semantic retrieval |\n","\n","My recommendation for a typical technical-document RAG system:\n","\n","1. Start with structure-aware recursive chunking.\n","2. Use token-based size limits.\n","3. Add moderate overlap.\n","4. Add reranking.\n","5. Evaluate semantic chunking or parent-child retrieval if needed.\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"9c0e2062","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-03T18:29:04.314645Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-03T18:29:04.313334Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-03T18:29:04.332729Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-03T18:29:04.330853Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-03T18:29:04.314609Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.003398,"end_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.138089+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.134691+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# Evaluation\n","\n","One of the most important skills for an advanced RAG engineer is diagnosing failure using data instead of guessing.\n","\n","A chatbot that produces fluent answers is not necessarily a good RAG system.\n","\n","You need an evaluation dataset containing representative questions, expected answers, and the documents or passages that should support them.\n","\n","## Build a test dataset\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","```py\n","evaluation_data = [    \n","    {        \n","        \"question\": \"How long is the refund window?\",        \"expected_answer\": \"30 days\",\n","        \"relevant_chunk_ids\": [\n","            \"refund-policy-v1:chunk-0\"\n","        ],\n","    },{\n","        \"question\": \"How long does an approved refund take?\",        \"expected_answer\": \"5 business days\",\n","        \"relevant_chunk_ids\": [\n","        \"refund-policy-v1:chunk-1\"\n","        ],\n","    }\n","]\n","```\n","\n","The chunk IDs here are illustrative. Your actual evaluation dataset must reference real indexed chunks or source passages.\n","\n","Include a variety of cases:\n","\n","- Simple factual questions.\n","- Questions requiring multiple passages.\n","- Paraphrased questions.\n","- Exact identifiers and rare terms.\n","- Questions with no answer in the corpus.\n","- Ambiguous questions.\n","- Conflicting or outdated sources.\n","- Permission-sensitive questions.\n","- Long questions and conversational follow-ups.\n","\n","## Retrieval metrics\n","\n","| Metric       | Meaning                                                   |\n","| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n","| Precision\\@K | Fraction of the top K retrieved chunks that are relevant  |\n","| Recall\\@K    | Fraction of all relevant chunks found in the top K        |\n","| MRR          | Rewards retrieving the first relevant result near the top |\n","| nDCG\\@K      | Evaluates ranking quality, including graded relevance     |\n","| Hit Rate\\@K  | Whether at least one relevant chunk appears in the top K  |\n","\n","For a simple case, suppose there are four retrieved chunks and two are relevant.\n","\n","$$ \n","\\text{Precision@4} = \\frac{2}{4}=0.5\n","$$\n","\n","If there are four relevant chunks in the entire corpus and the retriever finds two:\n","\n","$$\n","\\text{Recall@4}=\\frac{2}{4}=0.5\n","$$\n","\n","High precision with low recall means the results are mostly relevant but may omit necessary evidence. High recall with low precision means the retriever finds the evidence but also returns much irrelevant material.\n","\n","For multi-passage questions, evaluate whether the retriever finds all necessary evidence, not merely one relevant chunk.\n","\n","## Generation metrics\n","\n","| Metric                      | What it measures                                                  |\n","| --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n","| Answer correctness          | Whether the answer matches the ground truth                       |\n","| Faithfulness / groundedness | Whether claims are supported by retrieved evidence                |\n","| Answer relevance            | Whether the response addresses the question                       |\n","| Citation precision          | Whether cited passages support the claims                         |\n","| Citation coverage           | Whether claims that require evidence are cited                    |\n","| Abstention quality          | Whether the system declines to answer when evidence is inadequate |\n","\n","Frameworks such as RAGAS and LangSmith can help automate parts of this evaluation. MongoDB's RAG evaluation tutorial also demonstrates evaluating embeddings, retrieval, generation, and the overall application separately.\n","\n","LLM-as-a-judge metrics are useful, but they are not ground truth. Validate them against human-reviewed examples, especially for high-impact applications.\n","\n","## An evaluation loop\n","\n","Use this sequence when improving a RAG application:\n","\n","```\n","Create a fixed evaluation dataset\n","    |\n","Measure baseline retrieval and answer quality\n","    |\n","Change one component: chunks, embeddings, top-K, or reranker\n","    |\n","Re-run the same evaluation set\n","    |\n","Keep changes that improve quality without unacceptable cost or latency\n","```\n","\n","Don't change five things simultaneously. If you do, you may not know which change caused the improvement or regression. \n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":12,"id":"dcd8744f","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.146869Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.146563Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-03T18:52:11.490071Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-03T18:52:11.489036Z"},"papermill":{"duration":13.350548,"end_time":"2026-11-03T18:52:11.492086+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T18:51:58.141538+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Collecting ragas\r\n","  Downloading ragas-0.4.3-py3-none-any.wh